= The Death of Thaddeus Stevens

News arrives from Lancaster of the passing of Congressman Thaddeus Stevens, who has succumbed to chronic stomach ailments and dropsy. His life's work in advancing the rights of all citizens, regardless of race or former condition of servitude, will long be remembered. Upon learning that his intended resting place was a whites-only cemetery, Stevens arranged instead to be buried in Lancaster's public cemetery, where no racial restrictions applied. Among those arriving to pay their respects are Sumner, Trumbull, Cameron and other prominent figures of the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican Party**</span>.

His death is mourned by the Freemen population and the <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radical Republicans**</span>. The Democratic New York World, however, denounces him as the "author of more evil and mischief than any other inhabitant of the globe since the end of the Civil War." Even in death, Stevens remains a divisive figure, though we can only hope that history will remember him for his lifelong struggle for equality.

{!<figure>!}
  {!<blockquote cite="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thaddeus_Stevens">!}
    {!<p>!}
      I repose in this quiet and secluded spot<br>
      Not from any natural preference for solitude<br>
      But, finding other Cemeteries limited as to Race<br>
      by Charter Rules<br>
      I have chosen this that I might illustrate<br>
      in my death<br>
      The Principles which I advocated<br>
      through a long life;<br>
      EQUALITY OF MAN BEFORE HIS CREATOR
    {!</p>!}
  {!</blockquote>!}
  {!<figcaption>— Epitaph of <cite>Thaddeus Stevens</cite></figcaption>!}
{!</figure>!}

